    <DocumentationNarrative Id="source:cli:meta-mesh:app:command:run:narrative:example-example-meta-mesh-run-docs-operation:run-the-documentation-operation" DocumentationSubjectId="source:cli:meta-mesh:app:command:run" PreviousNarrativeId="source:cli:meta-mesh:app:command:run:narrative:guidance:execution-preflight">
      <Body>Run a modeled operation from its mesh workspace. The documentation mesh imports source workspaces, rebuilds the suite workspace, validates it, and renders the static site.

```powershell
cd MetaDocs\Docs\Documentation.MetaMesh
meta-mesh validate --operation regenerate-public-docs
meta-mesh run --operation regenerate-public-docs
```</Body>
      <Origin>Authored</Origin>
      <ReviewStatus>Current</ReviewStatus>
      <Slot>Example.example:meta-mesh:run-docs-operation</Slot>
      <Title>Run the documentation operation</Title>
    </DocumentationNarrative>
    <DocumentationNarrative Id="source:cli:meta-mesh:app:command:run:narrative:guidance:execution-preflight" DocumentationSubjectId="source:cli:meta-mesh:app:command:run" PreviousNarrativeId="source:cli:meta-mesh:app:command:run:narrative:example-example-meta-mesh-run-docs-operation:run-the-documentation-operation">
      <Body>`meta-mesh run --operation &lt;name&gt;` validates the selected operation before starting its steps. It resolves every workspace and token used by that operation, checks environment variables and executable paths, verifies working directories and permissions, and confirms that the step plan is runnable.

Use `meta-mesh validate --operation &lt;name&gt;` when you want the same preflight without executing child processes.</Body>
